content.thinking:
Found the root cause. In `CardRow.tsx`, the `load()` function uses a `for...of` loop with `await` inside it to fetch stats for each project sequentially:

```js
for (const p of projData) {
    const statsRes = await fetch(`/api/projects/${p.id}/stats`);
    const statsData = await statsRes.json();
    map[p.id] = statsData;
}
setStatsMap(map);
```

This means each stats request waits for the previous one to complete before starting. With 8 projects, each taking ~350ms, the total time is ~2.8 seconds. If these were fetched in parallel (e.g., with `Promise.all`), they'd all complete in ~350ms.
Also, `setStatsMap` only fires once every request resolves, so the UI shows nothing until the last project's stats come back. The network timing confirms the sequential pattern — each project's stats call starts right as the previous one ends, in a steady chain of ~350ms-long requests.
